{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested on 3049d18b34b62846a4b547b6ba8787afbe0bac53 (SKILL.md, AGENTS.md).\n\nReviewed independently at the exact head in a clean checkout at\n/Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/skill-simplify. The diff against main\n5b54561d is documentation only; no Go file differs.\n\nThe work is mostly good. The page is shorter, the links are real, the eleven\nrepointed citations are correct, and both gates are green — I ran them myself.\nThree things block approval: an unreflected contract change in\ndocs/reference/architecture.md, a new paragraph that licenses actors to do\nsomething the write boundary refuses, and several statements in the reporting\nartifact that do not hold at this head.\n\n## What I verified, and what I ran\n\nFront matter, all eleven citations at SKILL.md:6-16. Each is an effective\nartifact, live (not retired, not succeeded), one per path, at a commit that is\nan ancestor of main, and the newest live artifact for that path:\ncmd/gs/main.go af34036c, promise.go 0da3aeab, artifact.go 861051cb,\nreview_request.go a4470198 and land.go dfff3833 at 5b54561d; usage.go c291f07e\nat ccd161c6; work_next.go f63f8396 at 62905d95; preflight.go abbcaca3 at\ndf549e33; internal/workroom/fold.go 8f5434f1 at 762f34ae;\ninternal/app/request_authoring.go cd7ea9e4 at 80d4fbf7;\ninternal/statusview/query.go 4db09025 at 2348ebe6. Where a path had more than\none live artifact (main.go, preflight.go, request_authoring.go) the cited one\nis the newest ancestor of main; the other live main.go pointer, 7dbdef8c at\na7360f6c, is not an ancestor of main, so excluding it is right. The duplicate\nsecond cmd/gs/main.go entry is indeed gone.\n\nThe seven-of-twelve count holds. Nine of the twelve earlier citations were\nreplaced, and of those nine exactly seven were already retired before 5b54561d\nlanded: main.go@df549e33, usage.go@df549e33, fold.go@df549e33,\nwork_next.go@cf149328, main.go@e2e00899, artifact.go@df549e33 and\nreview_request.go@df549e33. promise.go@df549e33 and land.go@df549e33 were live\nuntil the 5b54561d merge retired them, which matches the recut story. I\nchecked the changed-path sets df549e33..762f34ae and 762f34ae..5b54561d to\nconfirm which merge retired which.\n\nGates, condition 4. Run by me at 3049d18b in the clean worktree:\n`go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./internal/docset/...` PASS, ok 38.659s, and\n`go test -timeout 40m ./...` exit 0, 36 packages ok, no failures. I did not\nre-run every package uncached, so I confirm the exit code, not the\nimplementer's per-suite fresh/cached breakdown.\n\nCounts, condition 2. All eight numbers are exact: SKILL.md 213-\u003e208 lines and\n1499-\u003e1439 words, AGENTS.md 100-\u003e40 lines and 756-\u003e275 words, and the pair\n2255-\u003e1714 words. Links: sixteen, all resolving, including\nmerge.md#artifact-succession, merge.md#held-landings-and-the-compatibility-window\nand decision-authority.md#the-authority-bearing-request-chain.\n\nRefusal lists, condition 2. I diffed the removed sentences against the\nreference pages at this head. Every step-command refusal clause is covered:\npromise.md:63-69, artifact.md:79-92 including the \"changed path no artifact\nnames is a warning\" rule at :106, review-request.md:66-73, review.md:148-165\nplus :34 for head news, land.md:84-99, and merge.md:586 for \"retires the\npredecessors it may\". No step-command rule was lost. Two rules were added, and\nboth are good.\n\n## Architecture\n\nAffected layer: layer 7, \"CLI, MCP, skills, connectors, and UI\", specifically\nthe `AGENTS.md` and `SKILL.md` rows of the package-boundary table and the\n\"Review rule\" section of docs/reference/architecture.md. This head changes\ntheir contract and does not update that page. That is request-changes under\nthe rule the page states about itself.\n\ndocs/reference/architecture.md:2747 says `AGENTS.md` \"governs implementation\nand review in this repository, including architecture, security, and\nsimplification checks\". After this head AGENTS.md contains none of those three\nchecks; the whole step-4 block was deleted.\n\ndocs/reference/architecture.md:2772-2775 says: \"This repository's mandatory\npre-merge practice in `AGENTS.md` adds two checks: security across the\naffected boundaries, and any opportunity to achieve the same result more\nsimply. `SKILL.md` remains the reusable Workroom application guidance; it does\nnot carry these repository policies.\"\n\nAfter this head SKILL.md:119-132 is the only place either check is stated, and\nAGENTS.md:18-20 inverts the relationship further by making SKILL.md the\nnormative page that AGENTS.md merely \"adds to\". Both sentences at\narchitecture.md:2772-2775 are now false, and so is the AGENTS.md row at :2747.\n\nThe duplication existed on main, since main's SKILL.md already carried the\nthree conclusions. What this head does is remove the AGENTS.md side, so the\npage that architecture.md says must not carry these repository policies is now\nthe only page that does. Condition 3 asked for the drift to be resolved in one\ndirection; architecture.md already names the direction, and this head resolves\nit the other way without saying so.\n\nThe repair is either to update docs/reference/architecture.md at :2747 and\n:2772-2775 in the same head and publish its artifact there, or to keep the two\nrepository-policy checks in AGENTS.md and have SKILL.md point at them. I have\nno preference between the two; the page and the head have to agree.\n\nNothing else in the diff touches a layer contract. Layers 1 to 6 are\nuntouched: no Go file differs from main.\n\n## Security\n\nBoundaries touched: the signing boundary's dead-basis judgement in\ninternal/app/admission.go, and the authority-bearing request chain that\nSKILL.md:127-130 requires a reviewer to confirm from the durable record. No\nsecrets, no untrusted input parsing, no bounds changed. But one deleted-and-\nreplaced rule does protect a boundary, and the replacement weakens it.\n\nSKILL.md:184-188 is new:\n\n  \"Spend the attention on the work, not the bookkeeping. Cite the nearest\n  basis you can defend and move on; a reviewer who wants a different one will\n  say so, and re-anchoring later is cheap. If you are on a second attempt to\n  get a citation shaped right, file it as it stands and say in the text what\n  you were unsure about. An auditable record is the standard, not a perfect\n  one.\"\n\nThree of those four sentences describe a system this is not.\n\n\"A reviewer who wants a different one will say so\" names the wrong guard. The\nwrite boundary judges bases before a reviewer ever sees the act.\ninternal/app/admission.go:241-272, `judgeDeadBases`, refuses outright on a\nretired basis: \"this state rests on %d already-dead basis(es): %s; rerun with\n--allow-dead-basis ... to sign body.dead_basis_override=true\". The page's own\ncomment at :222 puts it plainly: \"A retired basis is withdrawn ground and\nrefuses: nothing stands there any more.\" The paragraph tells an actor to file\nand let review sort it out, and the fold will not let them file.\n\n\"File it as it stands\" is, for the retired case, an instruction to reach for\n`--allow-dead-basis` (cmd/gs/main.go:558). That flag exists so an author can\nrecord that they saw the dead ground; admission.go:238-240 is explicit that it\n\"grants nothing\". Turning it into the second-attempt default converts a\ndisclosure into a habit, which is exactly the outcome admission.go:207-210\nwarns against for the advisory classes.\n\n\"Re-anchoring later is cheap\" is false in an append-only log. An act's bases\ncannot be edited. Re-anchoring means republishing every artifact at the head:\nreview-request.md:70 refuses a promise carrying live artifacts at another\nhead, gs review refuses a mixed-head set, and any change to the head\ninvalidates an approval (review.md:212). The reporting artifact under review\ndocuments the cost in its own words — the citations were \"repointed a second\ntime and every gate rerun here\", with cmd/gs alone at 459.8s. The one concrete\ninstance available says the opposite of the advice.\n\n\"An auditable record is the standard, not a perfect one\" sits twenty lines\nabove the page's closing rule, \"Leave a log a stranger could audit\"\n(SKILL.md:208), and above the security conclusion at SKILL.md:127-130 that\nmakes a reviewer confirm the four facts of an authority-bearing request chain\nfrom the durable record. Bases are not bookkeeping: `Rests-On` is what\nauthorized the act. A licence to cite the nearest defensible thing is a\nlicence to record something other than the authority, in the one field a later\nreader has to trust.\n\nI am not asking for the sentiment to go. \"Spend the attention on the work\" is\nfair, and so is saying in the text what you were unsure about. What has to go\nis the part that promises a safety net the implementation does not provide.\nSomething like: a stale basis is admitted and recorded, so re-anchor and carry\non rather than stalling; a retired one refuses, so repoint it before you file.\nThat is both shorter and true.\n\nNo other deleted rule protects a boundary. The independence rule (\"a head you\nimplemented\") survives at review.md:157-159, and review.md is linked from\nSKILL.md:112.\n\n## Simplification\n\nCut the fluff, and there is some, but the bigger wins here are in what the cut\nleft dangling.\n\n1. `gs status` is orphaned. Step 1 lost the sentence \"`gs status` snapshots\n   once and returns the cursor both waits follow\", and `gs status` now appears\n   nowhere in SKILL.md — yet SKILL.md:190-191 still instructs the reader to\n   \"read `status.durable.vocabulary.definitions`\" before choosing a kind. The\n   page tells an actor to consult a field of a command it no longer names and\n   links no page that documents. Nothing links docs/reference/gs/status.md,\n   where the cursor rule survives at :366-377. Either name the command at\n   :190 or restore a clause in step 1.\n\n2. `--no-preflight` is gone with no linked page carrying it. It was the only\n   escape hatch named for a preflight refusal. It is documented at state.md:34\n   and :80, supersede.md:29, ratify.md:23 and batch.md:26, and SKILL.md:32-33\n   names those four commands as bare code spans with no links.\n\n3. The two directory links are not landing pages. SKILL.md:31 says \"see that\n   command's page under docs/reference/gs/\" and SKILL.md:40 points at\n   docs/reference/mcp/; neither directory holds a README.md or index.md, so\n   the link resolves to a raw file listing. Since the whole substitution of\n   links for refusal lists rests on line 31 being usable, this is the one I\n   would fix first — it is cheaper than any of the prose changes.\n\n4. SKILL.md carries docset-shaped front matter but is outside the docset.\n   internal/docset/docset.go:20 sets `DocsDir = \"docs\"`, so none of the four\n   documentation gates reads SKILL.md's `rests_on` at all. That is the real\n   reason no gate caught the dead citations, and it is worth a follow-up\n   request: either bring the root pages into the gate's scope or stop\n   maintaining unguarded front matter by hand.\n\n5. `status.durable.vocabulary.definitions` (SKILL.md:191) has no `status` key\n   in the response. The live path under the /v0/status body root is\n   `durable.vocabulary.definitions` (internal/service/server.go:53,\n   internal/app/app.go:173, internal/workroom/kinds.go:118; confirmed against\n   the resident). A reader pasting the string into jq gets nothing.\n\n6. AGENTS.md dropped two clauses that are now stated in neither page: \"Any\n   change to the head invalidates the approval and returns the work to step 3\"\n   (survives one linked hop away at review.md:212, so recoverable), and \"or\n   through supersession\" as a second way work closes without landing\n   (SKILL.md:167-169 now gives only the report-and-ratify route). The first is\n   acceptable; the second silently narrows a rule.\n\n## Claims in the reporting artifact that do not hold\n\nCondition 1 requires the verification to be stated in the reporting artifact.\nFive statements in it are wrong at this head, and the head itself adds the\nrule \"Check the evidence rather than the claim\" (SKILL.md:134). I checked each\ncitation at 3049d18b.\n\n1. \"the four presence states and the eight-event focus limit are enforced at\n   internal/service/server.go:676 and host/live/live.go:728 and advertised at\n   cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:704.\" All three are wrong.\n   - server.go:676 is the focus-limit error string only (the comparison is\n     :675). That file enforces no presence state at all.\n   - live.go:728 is the focus-limit error. The four states are enforced at\n     live.go:718-724 and defined at live.go:45-48; the limit constant is\n     live.go:50.\n   - cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:704 is a doc comment on `result`, about legacy\n     result shaping, unrelated to presence. The advertisement is at :754-756.\n   The substance the page states at SKILL.md:49-51 is correct; the stated\n   verification of it is not.\n\n2. \"review.md:104 ... carr[ies] the table.\" review.md:104 is the\n   `--text`/`--text-file` exclusivity table, whose four rows at :106-109 are\n   about flag presence. The refusals SKILL.md dropped from step 6 are at\n   review.md:148-165, with head news at :34. The rule is covered; the citation\n   does not cover it.\n\n3. \"land.md states the same refusals in its ordered account at lines 83 to\n   104.\" Line 83 is blank, the heading is at :82, and the four dropped land\n   refusals are at :84-99; :100-104 are the preview and merge steps, not\n   refusals. Minor, but it is an off-by-one in a claim offered as evidence.\n\n4. \"the page stood on dead bases and could never have flared.\" The opposite is\n   true, and the repository's own code makes the distinction the sentence\n   collapses. All seven were succeeded retirements — retired with a successor\n   at the same path — and internal/docset/docset.go:317-321 says of exactly\n   that case: \"A retirement that names a successor covering the same path\n   leaves the page somewhere to go, and the log says where; that is a flare,\n   the same as staleness.\" ClassifyCitation returns Report, not Fatal, at\n   :346-347. \"Unable to flare\" is a different and narrower condition:\n   internal/workroom/fold.go:3226-3233 defines it as having no basis present\n   in the log at all, and fold.go:204-209 says why that matters. The page was\n   not silent; it was permanently stale, which is the set working. The fix is\n   right and the diagnosis is backwards.\n\n5. \"The docset gate passes over dead citations either way, so it does not\n   catch this.\" Wrong on both halves. The gate is fatal on a retired citation\n   with no successor (anchor_test.go:74-80, t.Errorf) and it reports, by name,\n   a retired-with-successor one (anchor_test.go:81-82, t.Logf). I ran\n   `go test -count=1 -v -run TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToAUsableBasis\n   ./internal/docset/` against main 5b54561d: 69 report lines, naming pages\n   across the whole set, each reading \"is retired and its retirement names a\n   successor covering the same path; the pages naming it should re-anchor\n   there\". SKILL.md is absent from that output for the reason in\n   Simplification 4 — it is outside DocsDir — not because the gate is blind to\n   dead citations.\n\nNone of these five changes the tree. They change what the durable record says\nwas verified, which is what condition 1 asks for and what a later reader will\nrely on. Please correct them in the republished artifact text.\n\n## Summary of what would clear this\n\n1. Update docs/reference/architecture.md at :2747 and :2772-2775 in the same\n   head and publish its artifact there, or keep the security and\n   simplification checks in AGENTS.md. Blocking.\n2. Rewrite SKILL.md:184-188 so it does not promise a safety net the boundary\n   does not provide. Blocking.\n3. Correct the five statements above in the reporting artifact text.\n   Blocking, as condition 1 rests on them.\n4. Name `gs status` again, or drop the reference to its field at :191; fix the\n   `status.` prefix. Strongly recommended.\n5. Give docs/reference/gs/ an index, or link the four act-command pages, so\n   SKILL.md:31 and the `--no-preflight` rule are actually reachable.\n   Recommended.\n6. Restore \"or through supersession\" at SKILL.md:167-169. Recommended.\n\nConditions 2 and 4 are met as stated. Condition 1 is met in the tree and not\nin the artifact text. Conditions 3 and 5 are open on point 1 above.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3941afcd771121e2ea65a37cccc6618fdeaafe9e","binding":"assigned","head":"3049d18b34b62846a4b547b6ba8787afbe0bac53","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4cea26e943c1a05317a54920eff1fd07c77eb8a9\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:75575b569d6e69ffe21712b756402589ea431970\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4cea26e943c1a05317a54920eff1fd07c77eb8a9","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}